{
  "id": "industrialisation-in-southern-africa",
  "title": "Industrialisation in Southern Africa: Sugar and Indentured Labour",
  "term": 1,
  "summary": "How the demand from British industry led to sugar plantations in Natal, why indentured labourers were brought from India, their working conditions, and Gandhi's early resistance in South Africa.",
  "notes": [
    { "type": "heading", "text": "Links between Britain and southern Africa" },
    { "type": "paragraph", "text": "British industry needed raw materials, and growing British cities wanted cheap food and luxuries such as **sugar**. The colony of **Natal** (today KwaZulu-Natal), which Britain took over in 1843, had a warm, wet climate ideal for growing **sugar cane**. From the 1850s, settlers started large sugar **plantations** along the coast." },
    { "type": "heading", "text": "The labour problem" },
    { "type": "paragraph", "text": "Plantation owners needed many workers for the hard work of cutting cane. Most Zulu men did not want to work on plantations for low wages: they had their own land, cattle and crops and did not need wage work. Planters therefore looked to another British colony, **India**." },
    { "type": "definition", "term": "Indentured labour", "text": "A system in which a worker signs a contract (an indenture) to work for a particular employer for a fixed number of years, usually five, in return for passage, food, housing and low wages." },
    { "type": "example", "title": "Why indentured labour?", "problem": "Why did Natal's sugar planters bring workers from India from 1860?", "steps": ["Sugar needs a lot of hard work for low pay.","Most Zulu men had land and cattle and did not need to work on plantations.","India was under British rule, and it had poor people willing to sign contracts."], "answer": "Planters could not find enough local workers, so they imported indentured workers." },
    { "type": "heading", "text": "Indentured labourers from India" },
    { "type": "list", "items": [
      "The first ship, the **Truro**, arrived in Durban on **16 November 1860**.",
      "Between 1860 and 1911 about **152 000** Indians came to Natal as indentured labourers.",
      "Many came from poor areas in south and north India, hoping to escape poverty and famine.",
      "After five years they could re-indenture, return to India, or stay as free workers; after ten years they were promised free passage home or a piece of land.",
      "Later, **passenger Indians** came at their own cost as traders and merchants."
    ] },
