t x='37.6' y='101.1' width='47.8' height='16.5' rx='3' fill='rgba(255,255,255,0.8)' stroke='none' stroke-width='0'/><text x='61.5' y='113.1' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle' fill='#1f2933'>AFRICA</text></svg>", "caption": "The sea route from India to Natal. The first ship, the Truro, reached Durban in 1860." },
    { "type": "heading", "text": "Working and living conditions" },
    { "type": "table", "headers": ["Promised", "Reality for many workers"], "rows": [
      ["Fair wages", "Very low wages (about 10 shillings a month), with deductions for being ill or absent"],
      ["Decent housing", "Crowded barracks with little privacy or sanitation"],
      ["Reasonable working hours", "Long hours in the fields and sugar mills, sometimes through the night at harvest"],
      ["Medical care", "Poor medical care; diseases spread easily"],
      ["Protection by law", "Workers who left the plantation without a pass could be arrested; some were beaten"]
    ] },
    { "type": "passage", "title": "Source B: A summary of historians' views (written for this lesson)", "text": "The indenture system was described by some critics at the time as 'a new system of slavery'. Workers were tied to one employer and could be punished for leaving, yet many also saw it as a chance to escape debt and hunger in India and build a new life." },
    { "type": "heading", "text": "Mohandas Gandhi and passive resistance" },
    { "type": "paragraph", "text": "**Mohandas Gandhi**, a young lawyer, arrived in Natal in **1893**. After being thrown off a first-class train compartment at Pietermaritzburg because of his skin colour, he began to fight discrimination. In 1894 he helped found the **Natal Indian Congress**. He developed **satyagraha** (passive resistance): non-violent refusal to obey unjust laws. In 1913 he led a strike and march of thousands of Indian workers against a £3 tax on former indentured labourers, which was later abolished." },
    { "type": "example", "title": "What passive resistance looks like", "problem": "Using Gandhi's campaign in the Transvaal, explain what passive resistance is.", "steps": ["Passive resistance means disobeying an unjust law peacefully and accepting the punishment.","A law required Indians to carry registration certificates.","In 1908, protesters publicly burned their certificates and many went to jail."], "answer": "Peaceful law-breaking to show a law is unjust." },
    { "type": "heading", "text": "Legacy" },
    { "type": "paragraph", "text": "The sugar industry is still important to KwaZulu-Natal's economy, and Durban has one of the largest communities of Indian descent outside India. Indian South Africans have contributed greatly to the country's culture, business and the struggle for democracy." }
  ],
